The rule for conformance.kernel.recovery states: "The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text" [cfb66510a6ca9655]. However, fence content must parse as JSON to be scored directly from the fence; if it contains multiple objects (fence[ A B ]), recovery falls back to top-level object scanning [cd36f9e459490eb4]. In the input constructed from cell fence[ A B ] S, the fence contains objects A and B on separate lines, which does not parse as a single JSON object. The rule falls to the else clause: "the last top-level object in the text", which is B. The sentence S following the fence is prose and ignored. In the rewritten text, object B is removed from the fence, leaving only A. The fence now contains a single valid JSON object A. The rule prioritizes the fenced object, so A is scored. Therefore, the recovered object moves from B to A.